const e={note:"Latin is the working language of Western esotericism, and three cautions govern this hall’s currents. Pseudepigraphy is the normal condition of the corpus rather than an aberration: the alchemical works of Albertus, Aquinas, Llull and Basil Valentine are all posthumous attributions, the Summa perfectionis travelled for six centuries under an Arabic name it may never have had, and Agrippa’s celebrated fourth book was denounced as a forgery by his own student. Where a work’s authorship is disputed, both the traditional ascription and the philological reckoning are given. Second, much of what Latin esotericism knows it learned in translation from Arabic, and the vocabulary still shows it — alembic, alkali, elixir, alcohol, athanor. Third, a good deal of this material was condemned, and the condemnations are part of the record: the 1487 censure of Pico’s theses and the bulls against Llull are given with their dates rather than passed over.",themes:[{slug:"opus-magnum",title:"The Great Work in Latin",native:"Opus Magnum",translit:"opus magnum",summary:"Alchemy arrived in Latin as a translation out of Arabic and stayed for six centuries — long enough to build the vocabulary every later European language borrowed rather than invented.",paragraphs:["The beginning is a translation. Latin had no alchemy of its own; what it had, from the twelfth century, was a stream of Arabic books turned into Latin in Spain. The Liber de compositione alchemiae — a dialogue in which the hermit Morienus instructs the Umayyad prince Khalid ibn Yazid — carries a colophon dating its completion to February 1144 and naming Robert of Chester, and it is conventionally called the first alchemical book in the language; whether its Robert is the Robert of Ketton who made the first Latin Qur'an in 1143 is argued, and the colophon's authority has itself been questioned. With the books came the words. Alembicus, alkali, elixir, borax, tutia, alcohol, athanor and alchemia are Arabic naturalised in these decades — al-anbiq, al-qily, al-iksir, at-tannur — and the Latin laboratory has spoken Arabic ever since.","The corpus that grew on that footing is pseudepigraphic almost as a matter of habit. The Turba philosophorum, the crowd of philosophers, stages a conference of pre-Socratics under garbled names and is a Latin version of an Arabic work that Julius Ruska's edition of 1931 placed in the ninth or tenth century. The Summa perfectionis magisterii, the most systematic technical treatise the Middle Ages produced, travelled under the name of Geber — that is, Jabir ibn Hayyan — until William Newman's edition of 1991 argued it was composed in Latin in the later thirteenth century by Paul of Taranto, a Franciscan lector; if that is right, the outstanding Arabic book of Latin alchemy is a Latin original wearing an Arabic name. Arnald of Villanova, Albertus Magnus, Thomas Aquinas, Ramon Llull and Basil Valentine all acquired alchemical works after their deaths, and in several cases the pseudonymous corpus is larger and more influential than the genuine one.","The stages are borrowed too. Greek alchemy had counted colours — melanosis, leukosis, xanthosis, iosis in the tradition running through Zosimos — and Latin took them over as nigredo, albedo, citrinitas and rubedo: the blackening in which the matter putrefies and dies, the whitening that washes it, the yellowing, and the reddening in which the work is perfected. Citrinitas thins out of the literature from the fifteenth century, which is why later authors so often count three stages rather than four. Nothing about the sequence is standardised beyond the colours: the regimens between them are counted as seven by some authors and twelve by others, the same operation appears under different names in adjacent treatises, and the Rosarium philosophorum's twenty woodcuts of 1550 tell the whole thing as a marriage, a death and a resurrection without giving a single quantity.","The vocabulary was built to be difficult. Prima materia is the formless base said to underlie all bodies and to be recoverable from the meanest of them; the lapis philosophorum is described as lapis et non lapis, a stone and not a stone; and the cover-names — green lion, red king, our mercury, the doves of Diana — are deliberate, the texts openly enjoining obscurity on the ground that the art must not fall to the unworthy or the merely greedy. One caution belongs here. Solve et coagula, the pair of imperatives now quoted as the motto of the whole discipline, is not in the Emerald Tablet, which speaks instead of separating the earth from the fire and the subtle from the gross; the slogan belongs to the emblematic literature of the early seventeenth century, lettered around the Azoth figure printed at Frankfurt in 1613 under the name of Basil Valentine, and it is cited as scripture far more often than it is sourced.","By 1700 the whole of it was in print and in one language. Lazarus Zetzner's Theatrum Chemicum, six folio volumes gathered between 1602 and 1661, holds some two hundred treatises, and Jean-Jacques Manget's Bibliotheca Chemica Curiosa of 1702 about a hundred and forty more; Newton worked from these volumes and so, three centuries later, did Jung. What was going on in them is the field's live argument. The Jungian reading, which takes the operations as projected psychological process, dominated the middle of the twentieth century; William Newman and Lawrence Principe have argued since the 1990s that the texts describe real laboratory work in a discipline for which they revive the period term chymistry, and that the spiritual and the material readings were not alternatives to the men who wrote them. Both positions are held by serious scholars, and the corpus is large enough to supply evidence for each."],terms:[{native:"prima materia",translit:"prima materia",gloss:"first matter; the formless base of the Work, Aristotle's materia prima taken out of the schools and put into the crucible"},{native:"solve et coagula",translit:"solve et coagula",gloss:"dissolve and coagulate; the discipline's best-known slogan, and one that belongs to the seventeenth-century emblem books rather than to the Emerald Tablet it is usually credited to"},{native:"nigredo",translit:"nigredo",gloss:"blackening; the putrefaction that opens the Work, and the one stage every author agrees must come first"},{native:"citrinitas",translit:"citrinitas",gloss:"yellowing; the stage between white and red that quietly leaves the literature in the fifteenth century, turning a sequence of four colours into one of three"},{native:"proiectio",translit:"proiectio",gloss:"the casting of the finished powder upon molten metal — the last act of the Work, and the only one that could be publicly witnessed"},{native:"sigillum Hermetis",translit:"sigillum Hermetis",gloss:"the seal of Hermes: the fusing shut of the vessel's neck so that nothing can escape it, and the ancestor of the English phrase 'hermetically sealed'"}],texts:[{title:"Liber de compositione alchemiae (the Morienus dialogue)",note:"Colophon dated February 1144 and naming Robert of Chester; conventionally the first alchemical work in Latin, with both the date and the translator's identity open to question. Edited and translated by Lee Stavenhagen as A Testament of Alchemy (1974)."},{title:"Turba philosophorum",note:"A Latin translation of an Arabic dialogue placed by Julius Ruska's edition of 1931 in the ninth or tenth century; the pre-Socratics reconvened under corrupted names, and the oldest voice in the Latin corpus."},{title:"Summa perfectionis magisterii (pseudo-Geber)",note:"The most systematic technical treatise of medieval alchemy, with a corpuscular theory of matter. Edited by William R. Newman (1991), who argued for Paul of Taranto and a Latin original rather than a translation from Arabic."},{title:"Rosarium philosophorum (Frankfurt, 1550)",note:"An anthology of sayings from the older Latin authors, whose twenty woodcuts of the king and queen — meeting, bathing, joined, dead in the coffin, risen crowned — became the best-known images alchemy produced."},{title:"Theatrum Chemicum (Zetzner, 1602–1661) and J.-J. Manget, Bibliotheca Chemica Curiosa (Geneva, 1702)",note:"The two great folio libraries in which the whole corpus became one readable body; the form in which Newton, and later Jung, encountered nearly all of it."}]},{slug:"grimoria",title:"The Latin Grimoires",native:"Clavicula Salomonis",translit:"Clavicula Salomonis",summary:"Books of ritual magic written by clerics in the language of the Church, in which the operator confesses, fasts and hears Mass — and then commands spirits by the names of God.",paragraphs:["These books are in Latin because the men who wrote, copied and used them were the men who had Latin: priests, monks, notaries, physicians, university men. Richard Kieckhefer named the milieu a clerical underworld, and the phrase has stuck because the texts insist on it. The rites presuppose the sacraments — confession, fasting, sexual abstinence, the hearing of Mass, holy water, blessed chalk, virgin parchment — and the Church's own apparatus is what makes the operation work. The clearest single window is Clm 849 in the Bayerische Staatsbibliothek, a fifteenth-century handbook edited by Kieckhefer as Forbidden Rites (1997): illusions, compulsions, divination in a mirror or a boy's polished fingernail, and the untroubled assumption throughout that the operator is in orders.","The Ars notoria is the oldest of them, with manuscripts from the early thirteenth century. It offers prayers — some Latin, some in long strings of unintelligible words presented as Hebrew, Greek and Chaldean — together with figures called notae, diagrams of text and image to be inspected while the prayers are said, and it promises infused knowledge of grammar, dialectic, rhetoric, arithmetic and the rest without the labour of study. Julien Véronèse's critical edition of 2007 is the foundation of modern work on it. Aquinas rejected it in the Summa theologiae (II-II q. 96 a. 1) on the ground that God does not distribute learning by such means. Its most remarkable witness is John of Morigny's Liber florum celestis doctrine, an early fourteenth-century monk's account of having used the Ars notoria, been frightened by what came of it, and set out to compose a licit replacement — the nearest thing this literature has to a first-person testimony.","The Liber iuratus Honorii, the Sworn Book, is attributed to one Honorius of Thebes and opens with a frame story of a congress of masters assembled against papal persecution; the operator swears an oath limiting the number of copies and the sort of person they may pass to, whence the title. Its declared object is not wealth or love but the beatific vision — to see God in this life. Gösta Hedegård's edition of 2002 places the text in the thirteenth century, while the earliest surviving witnesses are of the fourteenth. It also transmits the figure called the sigillum Dei Aemeth, a complex seal of divine names, which surfaces three centuries later on the table John Dee used for his angel conversations: a demonstrable line of descent in a field where most lines are asserted rather than shown.","The Clavicula Salomonis is a family rather than a book. Latin, Italian and French versions circulate from the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries, related at a distance to the Greek Solomonic material known as the Hygromanteia; two books, the first on the operations, the second on the times, instruments, circles and pentacles they require. The Heptameron seu elementa magica, printed in 1559 under the name of Pietro d'Abano, who died in 1316, is a compact set of angelic conjurations for the days of the week and the seasons; the attribution is rejected, and the text is generally taken for a fifteenth-century compilation. The 1559 volume that carried it also carried the spurious Fourth Book of Occult Philosophy, and the two travelled together thereafter, so that much of what later readers called Agrippa and Abano is neither.","The transmission into modern hands runs through a peculiar channel. The demon list of the seventeenth-century Lemegeton descends from the Pseudomonarchia daemonum that Johann Weyer appended to the 1577 edition of De praestigiis daemonum — a catalogue printed by a man arguing that the spirits could do nothing of the kind, so that the best-known roster of demons in English reaches us through a sceptic's exhibit. S. L. MacGregor Mathers then edited the Clavicula in 1889 from British Museum manuscripts, most of them of the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries, silently omitting what he disapproved of, and the Golden Dawn's line inherited that text. What is described here is the shape of these books, what they promised, who owned them and how they were transmitted; their operations are not set out, and were compiled by their owners for nobody's use but their own."],terms:[{native:"clavicula",translit:"clavicula",gloss:"little key; the standard title-word of the Solomonic literature, a book presented as the key to an art rather than the art itself"},{native:"nota",translit:"nota",gloss:"a figure of the Ars notoria, combining text and diagram, to be looked at while its prayer is said; the source of the work's name"},{native:"coniuratio",translit:"coniuratio",gloss:"conjuration; literally a swearing-together — the spirit is bound by an oath on divine names rather than persuaded"},{native:"nigromantia",translit:"nigromantia",gloss:"the medieval spelling of necromantia, reshaped by a false etymology on niger, 'black'; how Greek divination by the dead became the black art"},{native:"pentaculum",translit:"pentaculum",gloss:"pentacle; a drawn figure of names and signs, worn or held, protective rather than compulsive in most of the Latin manuals"},{native:"sigillum Dei Aemeth",translit:"sigillum Dei Aemeth",gloss:"the seal of God, from the Liber iuratus, incorporating the Hebrew word emet, 'truth'; the same figure reappears on John Dee's table three centuries later"}],texts:[{title:"Ars notoria",note:"Manuscripts from the early thirteenth century; prayers, unintelligible names, and figures promising infused knowledge of the liberal arts. Critical edition by Julien Véronèse, L'Ars notoria au Moyen Âge (2007)."},{title:"Liber iuratus Honorii (the Sworn Book of Honorius)",note:"Edited by Gösta Hedegård (2002), who places the text in the thirteenth century against fourteenth-century witnesses; its object is the vision of God in this life, and it transmits the sigillum Dei Aemeth."},{title:"Munich, Bayerische Staatsbibliothek, Clm 849",note:"A fifteenth-century necromancer's handbook, edited and translated by Richard Kieckhefer as Forbidden Rites (1997); the fullest single view of what such a book contained and who used it."},{title:"Clavicula Salomonis",note:"A family of texts in Latin, Italian and French from the fifteenth century onward. Mathers's English edition of 1889 was made from British Museum manuscripts of the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries and is expurgated without notice."},{title:"Heptameron seu elementa magica (printed 1559)",note:"Ascribed to Pietro d'Abano, dead in 1316, and not his; probably a fifteenth-century compilation. Printed with the pseudonymous Fourth Book of Occult Philosophy, with which it thereafter travelled."}]},{slug:"magia-naturalis",title:"Natural Magic",native:"Magia Naturalis",translit:"magia naturalis",summary:"Three men in fifty years argued that some magic works on nature alone and is therefore lawful — and each of the three got into trouble anyway.",paragraphs:["Marsilio Ficino finished De vita libri tres in 1489. The first two books are medicine for scholars and for old age; the third, De vita coelitus comparanda — on obtaining life from the heavens — grew out of his commentary on Plotinus, Enneads IV.3.11, the passage about the ancient priests who drew soul into statues. Its mechanism is the spiritus, the fine vapour by which soul was held to move body; there is a spiritus mundi answering to it in the world at large; and therefore things belonging to a planet — plants, metals, scents, colours, images, songs — can be gathered so as to draw that planet's influence into one's own spirit. The whole apparatus is addressed, in the first instance, to the melancholic scholar born under Saturn, which is to say to Ficino himself.","He knew what he was handling. He appended an Apologia to the book, distinguished between drawing down natural influence and addressing intelligences, and repeatedly says of talismanic images that he reports rather than approves them. Whether the distinction survives inside his own system is the question of D. P. Walker's Spiritual and Demonic Magic from Ficino to Campanella (1958), and Walker's answer is that it does not entirely. The pressure on him was real: Ficino was an ordained priest from 1473, and the Asclepius — which he had not translated, since it had come down in an anonymous Latin version made in the fourth century — contains the god-making passage that Augustine had damned. His letters of these years show a man expecting to be accused.","Giovanni Pico della Mirandola went further and faster. On 7 December 1486 the Roman press of Eucharius Silber issued his nine hundred theses, among them twenty-six magical conclusions according to his own opinion, which include the assertion that no science more certainly assures us of Christ's divinity than magic and Kabbalah, and the division on which every subsequent defence would try to stand: that magic operating through the virtues of created natures is the practical part of natural science and lawful, while magic that petitions powers is not. Innocent VIII stopped the disputation in February 1487; a commission censured thirteen theses; Pico's Apologia made matters worse and drew a bull in August condemning all nine hundred. He fled, was arrested in France, was released through Lorenzo de' Medici's intercession, and was absolved by Alexander VI in 1493. He died in 1494, aged thirty-one; an exhumation and toxicological study reported in 2007 found arsenic at levels consistent with poisoning, which is a suggestion and not a verdict.","Heinrich Cornelius Agrippa drafted De occulta philosophia in 1509 and 1510 and sent it to Trithemius, who replied on 8 April 1510 with praise and the advice to speak of such matters only among friends. It circulated in manuscript for twenty years; Book I was printed in 1531 and the three books complete at Cologne in 1533, much enlarged. Their architecture is the Neoplatonic ascent through three worlds — elemental, celestial, ceremonial — and their achievement is that they turn a scattered inheritance into a curriculum, with the tables, magic squares, seals and alphabets that make it usable. In 1530, between the drafting and the printing, Agrippa had published De incertitudine et vanitate scientiarum, which derides the occult sciences along with law, medicine and theology, and the 1533 edition prints a retraction beside the magic it teaches. Whether that is recantation, self-protection or rhetorical performance has been argued from Charles Nauert to Paola Zambelli and Marc van der Poel without resolution.","The fourth book is not his, and this needs saying plainly because so much rests on it. In 1559 a Liber quartus de occulta philosophia appeared, offering the conjuring operations the three genuine books had described in principle but never set out, bound with the Heptameron and a treatise on geomancy. Johann Weyer, who had lived in Agrippa's household as his famulus and knew both the man and his papers, denounced it as a forgery in De praestigiis daemonum (1563); no serious scholarship has since defended the attribution. It nevertheless became the most consequential thing ever published under the name — Robert Turner's English version of 1655 carried it into the Anglophone tradition — so that a great deal of what later magic credits to Agrippa comes from the one book he did not write. That the pupil who exposed the forgery is also the physician who argued that accused witches were sick rather than guilty is not a coincidence worth passing over."],terms:[{native:"magia naturalis",translit:"magia naturalis",gloss:"natural magic; in Pico's formula the practical part of natural science, working through the virtues of created things and therefore held to be lawful"},{native:"goetia",translit:"goetia",gloss:"the Greek word for sorcery, taken into Latin as the name of everything natural magic insists it is not — the petitioning or compelling of spirits"},{native:"spiritus mundi",translit:"spiritus mundi",gloss:"the spirit of the world; the fine intermediate substance through which, in Ficino's account, celestial influence reaches bodies, answering to the spiritus in the human being"},{native:"virtus occulta",translit:"virtus occulta",gloss:"hidden virtue; the scholastic term for a power not reducible to the manifest qualities of hot, cold, wet and dry — the phrase from which 'occult philosophy' is built"},{native:"prisca theologia",translit:"prisca theologia",gloss:"the ancient theology; Ficino's name for a single revealed wisdom descending from Hermes through Orpheus and Pythagoras to Plato and agreeing with Christianity"},{native:"imagines",translit:"imagines",gloss:"images; astrological talismans engraved under an elected sky. Ficino discusses them at length and says he reports rather than approves them"}],texts:[{title:"Marsilio Ficino, De vita libri tres (Florence, 1489)",note:"Book III, De vita coelitus comparanda, grew out of his commentary on Plotinus, Enneads IV.3.11. Edited and translated by Carol Kaske and John Clark (1989)."},{title:"Giovanni Pico della Mirandola, Conclusiones nongentae (Rome, 7 December 1486)",note:"Nine hundred theses, including twenty-six magical conclusions of his own opinion. Edited, translated and annotated by S. A. Farmer, Syncretism in the West (1998)."},{title:"Agrippa, De occulta philosophia libri tres (Cologne, 1533)",note:"Drafted 1509–10, sent to Trithemius, printed complete in 1533 with a retraction bound in beside it. Critical edition by V. Perrone Compagni (1992)."},{title:"Agrippa, De incertitudine et vanitate scientiarum (1530)",note:"The counter-book, mocking the occult sciences with all the others; the reason the 1533 volume can be read three ways, and the reason no reading has won."},{title:"Johann Weyer, De praestigiis daemonum (1563)",note:"Agrippa's former famulus denounces the Fourth Book of Occult Philosophy as spurious, and argues that accused witches are ill rather than criminal."}]},{slug:"ars-magna",title:"The Combinatory Art",native:"Ars Magna",translit:"Ars Magna",summary:"A Majorcan layman built a machine of revolving paper wheels to prove Christianity by reason, denied that transmutation was possible, and was made the patron saint of alchemy anyway.",paragraphs:["Ramon Llull was born about 1232 on Majorca, a generation after the Aragonese conquest, on an island where Catalan, Arabic and Hebrew were all spoken. He was a courtier and a married man until a conversion he dated to about 1263, followed by years of study and by the illumination on Mount Randa that his Life places at the origin of the Art. He wrote in Catalan and in Latin, and in Arabic, of which nothing survives. The purpose was single and never varied: an art that would demonstrate the articles of the Christian faith by necessary reasons, from premises Muslims and Jews already granted, so that no appeal to Scripture was required. He petitioned popes and kings for forty years for language schools and missions, and crossed to North Africa himself more than once.","The Art went through successive versions, from the Ars compendiosa inveniendi veritatem of about 1274 to the Ars generalis ultima of 1308 and its abridgement, the Ars brevis, of the same year. In the late form nine letters, B to K, each stand simultaneously for a divine dignity — bonitas, magnitudo, duratio, potestas, sapientia, voluntas, virtus, veritas, gloria — and for a relation, a question, a subject, a virtue and a vice. The figures are diagrams: a circle of the dignities with every pair joined by a chord; a triangular table of the thirty-six pairs those nine terms yield; and a fourth figure of three concentric circles of parchment, the inner two revolving, so that triples of letters are brought into alignment by turning. The point is that the combinations are generated rather than thought of, and the operator's work is to read out what the wheels have produced.","Llull's orthodoxy was contested in his own century and after it. Nicholas Eymerich, inquisitor of Aragon, attacked him in the Directorium inquisitorum of 1376 and produced a bull of Gregory XI condemning a hundred articles drawn from his works; the Lullists denied the bull's authenticity at the time, and the question has never been finally settled. He died in 1315 or 1316. The tradition tells of stoning by a crowd at Bugia and death aboard ship within sight of Majorca; the earliest life, the Vita coaetanea dictated in 1311, ends before any such event, and the martyrdom is generally held to be a later accretion. His cult was eventually confirmed, but he has never been canonised.","Llull rejected the possibility of metallic transmutation in his authentic works, and from the 1330s an enormous alchemical corpus grew under his name regardless. The Testamentum, whose colophon places its completion in London in 1332, the Codicillus, and the Liber de secretis naturae seu de quinta essentia are its principal texts; Michela Pereira's catalogue of 1989 runs to well over a hundred items, none of them his. That corpus absorbed the quintessence programme of John of Rupescissa and became one of the two or three most reprinted bodies of alchemical writing in Europe, so that a Renaissance reader who bought Lull bought pseudo-Lull, and the man who denied the art became its most cited authority. It is the sharpest illustration in this hall of what pseudepigraphy does to a tradition's memory of itself.","The Art itself had a second life among people with quite different aims. Agrippa wrote a commentary on the Ars brevis. Giordano Bruno rebuilt the wheels as an engine of memory and invention in De umbris idearum and De compendiosa architectura et complemento artis Lullii, both printed at Paris in 1582, replacing the dignities with images. Athanasius Kircher's Ars magna sciendi of 1669 is the baroque monument to the method. And in 1666 a twenty-year-old Leibniz published the Dissertatio de arte combinatoria, taking from Llull the ambition of an alphabet of human thoughts whose combinations could be computed, while judging Llull's own execution feeble. The word combinatoria is Leibniz's and not Llull's. The line from a missionary's paper wheels to the calculus ratiocinator is real, but it has been drawn far too straight; Paolo Rossi's Clavis universalis (1960) and Frances Yates's essays on Lullism are where the argument is actually conducted."],terms:[{native:"Ars magna",translit:"Ars magna",gloss:"the great art; Llull's own name for the method, distinct from the Ars brevis, the short abridgement he wrote to make it teachable"},{native:"dignitates",translit:"dignitates",gloss:"the divine dignities — goodness, greatness, duration, power, wisdom, will, virtue, truth, glory — the nine absolute principles the whole Art combines"},{native:"camera",translit:"camera",gloss:"a compartment; one cell of a Lullian figure, holding a single combination of letters and so a single proposition to be read out"},{native:"figura",translit:"figura",gloss:"figure; the diagrams themselves, including the fourth, whose concentric parchment circles turn to generate triples of terms"},{native:"ars combinatoria",translit:"ars combinatoria",gloss:"the combinatory art; Leibniz's phrase of 1666, not Llull's, and a reminder that the tradition's name for itself was fixed by an heir rather than a founder"},{native:"quinta essentia",translit:"quinta essentia",gloss:"fifth essence; the distilled incorruptible virtue of a body, a programme owed to John of Rupescissa and carried into Europe by the alchemical corpus falsely bearing Llull's name"}],texts:[{title:"Ramon Llull, Ars brevis (1308)",note:"The short form of the final Art, and the version through which most later readers met it: nine letters, the figures, and the rules for reading the combinations."},{title:"Vita coaetanea (dictated 1311)",note:"Llull's own account of his conversion and career, taken down by Carthusians at Paris; the earliest life, and it stops before the martyrdom the later tradition supplies."},{title:"Testamentum (dated 1332) and the pseudo-Lullian alchemical corpus",note:"Not Llull's, and larger than his genuine work in circulation. Catalogued by Michela Pereira, The Alchemical Corpus Attributed to Raymond Lull (1989)."},{title:"Athanasius Kircher, Ars magna sciendi sive combinatoria (Amsterdam, 1669)",note:"The seventeenth century's most elaborate reconstruction of the method, with its own alphabet of terms and its own revolving figures."},{title:"G. W. Leibniz, Dissertatio de arte combinatoria (Leipzig, 1666)",note:"Written at twenty; takes from Llull the ambition of a computable alphabet of thoughts while dismissing his execution, and gives the tradition the name it now goes by."}]},{slug:"hermetica-latina",title:"Hermes in Latin",native:"Hermes Trismegistus",translit:"Hermes Trismegistus",summary:"For eleven centuries Europe knew exactly one philosophical Hermetic book, and it was in Latin because the Greek had been lost.",paragraphs:["The Asclepius — the Perfect Discourse, in which Trismegistus instructs Asclepius, Tat and Ammon — survives whole only in an anonymous Latin translation; the Greek original is gone. That original is placed in the third or early fourth century: its closing prayer survives in Greek in the Mimaut papyrus of about 300, and Lactantius quotes the Greek in the years around 310. The Latin version is fixed between two ends, later than Lactantius, who did not use it, and earlier than Augustine, who attacks the Latin text by name in the City of God (VIII.23–26) between about 415 and 417. The manuscripts transmit it among the philosophical works of Apuleius and it was long printed as his, which it is not. In 1945 a Coptic version of chapters 21 to 29 came out of the ground at Nag Hammadi, confirming that the Latin translator had a real Greek book in front of him.","Three passages did the work. Magnum miraculum est homo — a great wonder is man — which Pico quotes in the opening paragraph of the oration now called On the Dignity of Man. The Lament for Egypt, foretelling that the gods will depart the land, that religion will become an empty story, and that the temples will fill with corpses. And the passage on statues, in which men make gods, drawing souls into images by herbs, stones, scents and rites, so that the images speak and foretell. That last is why Augustine attacks the book at length — and why it survived, since for most medieval readers Hermes existed chiefly inside a refutation. To know that there had been an Egyptian sage who wrote before Moses, a Latin reader needed only the City of God.","The medieval Latin Hermes is not one figure. Abu Ma'shar's account of three men of that name reached Latin through the twelfth-century translations, and the name attached itself to a technical literature — astrological, alchemical, talismanic — with no relation to the philosophical dialogues: the Liber Hermetis on the fifteen fixed stars, the Liber de sex rerum principiis of the twelfth century, and above all the Emerald Tablet, which travelled inside the pseudo-Aristotelian Secretum secretorum and was among the most copied things in Europe. The modern division of the corpus into philosophical and technical Hermetica is A.-J. Festugière's, it is a scholar's convenience, and it maps badly onto anything a medieval reader would have recognised as Hermes.","A Greek codex of the treatises reached Florence about 1460, carried from Macedonia by the monk Leonardo da Pistoia. Cosimo de' Medici, old and impatient, had Ficino set Plato aside and translate it first; he finished in the spring of 1463, and Cosimo read it before his death the following year. The version covers treatises I to XIV; the title Pimander belongs properly to the first alone. It was printed at Treviso in 1471, prefaced by Francesco Rolandello and printed by Gerardus de Lisa without Ficino's involvement, and it is that text, not his own, which Europe reprinted for a century. Ficino's preface set Hermes at the head of a prisca theologia running through Orpheus, Aglaophemus, Pythagoras and Philolaus to Plato. The treatises his manuscript lacked, XVI to XVIII, were rendered by Ludovico Lazzarelli and reached print in the early sixteenth century.","How completely the antiquity was believed can be read off a floor: about 1488 Hermes Trismegistus was inlaid in the marble pavement of Siena Cathedral and labelled a contemporary of Moses. In 1614 Isaac Casaubon, in the first of sixteen exercises against Baronius, took the claim apart from the inside — the Greek is late, it quotes the Septuagint, its vocabulary is that of Hellenistic philosophy — and placed the treatises in the Christian era. Frances Yates in 1964 made that the hinge of an epoch; the periodisation has since been heavily qualified, Wouter Hanegraaff and others observing that the alchemists carried on undisturbed, that Kircher wrote as though nothing had happened, and that the practical tradition had never depended on the date. Casaubon's dating has held. His corollary, that the texts owe Egypt nothing, has not: Mahé and Fowden argued for a real Egyptian substrate, and the demotic Book of Thoth, published in 2005, shows a native dialogue of the same shape. One last caution: 'Hermeticism' as the name of a tradition is a modern construction. There was no Hermetic church, and the ancient texts describe none."],terms:[{native:"Asclepius",translit:"Asclepius",gloss:"the Latin title of the Perfect Discourse; the one philosophical Hermetic book the West never lost, and the only complete witness to a Greek dialogue that is gone"},{native:"Pimander",translit:"Pimander",gloss:"properly the title of the first treatise alone; from 1471 the name Europe used for Ficino's whole collection, and for the Hermetic corpus as such"},{native:"Corpus Hermeticum",translit:"Corpus Hermeticum",gloss:"the modern name for the seventeen Greek treatises; no Renaissance reader used it, which is worth remembering when older sources are quoted"},{native:"Trismegistus",translit:"Trismegistus",gloss:"thrice-greatest; a Greek epithet rendering an Egyptian formula for Thoth, carried untranslated into Latin and never rendered into it"},{native:"magnum miraculum est homo",translit:"magnum miraculum est homo",gloss:"'a great wonder is man' — the Asclepius sentence Pico sets at the head of his oration, and the single most quoted line of Latin Hermetism"},{native:"prisca theologia",translit:"prisca theologia",gloss:"the ancient theology; Ficino's genealogy of one revelation running from Hermes through Orpheus, Aglaophemus, Pythagoras and Philolaus to Plato"}],texts:[{title:"Asclepius",note:"Fourth-century Latin, the Greek lost; edited by Nock and Festugière (1945–54) with the Corpus Hermeticum. Chapters 21–29 survive in Coptic at Nag Hammadi (NHC VI,8)."},{title:"Augustine, De civitate Dei VIII.23–26",note:"The attack on the god-making passage, written about 415–417; it fixes the terminus for the Latin translation and is how most medieval readers met Hermes at all."},{title:"Lactantius, Divinae institutiones I.6",note:"Quotes the Greek Hermetica about 310 and holds Trismegistus immeasurably ancient — the authority Ficino leaned on, and the reason the antiquity went unquestioned."},{title:"Ficino's Pimander (translated 1463; first printed Treviso, 1471)",note:"Treatises I–XIV, with the preface that set Hermes at the head of the prisca theologia. The 1471 printing, which Ficino did not oversee, is the text Europe actually read."},{title:"Isaac Casaubon, De rebus sacris et ecclesiasticis exercitationes XVI (London, 1614)",note:"The first exercise redates the Hermetica to the Christian era on linguistic grounds. The dating has held; the conclusion that the texts owe Egypt nothing has not."}]},{slug:"missa-et-notariacon",title:"The Mass and the Square",native:"SATOR AREPO TENET OPERA ROTAS",translit:"SATOR AREPO TENET OPERA ROTAS",summary:"Latin has no gematria, so its letter-mysticism had to be built out of arrangement instead — acrostics, palindromes, and the words of the liturgy carried out of doors.",paragraphs:["Begin with what is absent. In Hebrew and Greek every letter carries a number, so every word has a sum and sums can be compared; that single fact generates gematria, isopsephy, and centuries of argument. Latin has nothing of the kind. Seven letters bear number — I, V, X, L, C, D, M — and they do so as plain numerals with no relation to their place in the alphabet: I is one but the ninth letter, M is a thousand but the twelfth. No Latin word has a meaningful total. What Latin has in abundance instead is the acronym, because Roman epigraphy ran on drastic abbreviation: D M for the divine shades, I O M for Jupiter Best and Greatest, V S L M for a vow paid gladly and deservedly, H S E for here he lies. A culture trained to read three letters on a stone as a whole sentence has already acquired the habit that produces INRI and VITRIOL.","The liturgy supplied the material. The Roman rite was said in Latin, its Canon fixed and its words memorised by people who did not understand them, and its most charged sentence — hoc est enim corpus meum — was spoken quietly at the altar. Two features of the medieval Mass fed directly into the letter-mysticism this hall records. The prologue of John, In principio erat Verbum, was recited at the end of the rite as the Last Gospel and fixed there in the Missal of 1570; it was also the most-copied protective text of the Latin Middle Ages, carried on amulets and read over the sick. And the rite for dedicating a church, in the Pontificale Romanum, has the bishop trace the Greek and the Latin alphabets in ashes strewn across the floor in a diagonal cross — the building taken possession of by the letters themselves, both alphabets, laid out in full.","The best-known claim about the Mass's afterlife is also the weakest. In a sermon printed in 1694 John Tillotson, archbishop of Canterbury, wrote that the conjurors' hocus pocus was in all probability a corruption of hoc est corpus, in ridiculous imitation of the priests. The derivation is repeated everywhere and the lexicographers do not accept it: hocus pocus is attested in England as a juggler's name and patter by the 1620s, and Tillotson's account is a Protestant controversialist's guess about it seventy years later. The point is worth making because the underlying phenomenon is real and well documented — consecrated hosts stolen for charms, altar stones and vestments among the grimoires' apparatus, the Mass said over objects to be used — while the single piece of evidence everyone quotes for it is not evidence at all.","The SATOR square is the hall's own emblem, and nothing about it is settled. Twenty-five letters in a five-by-five grid, reading the same left to right, right to left, top to bottom and bottom to top; two examples were sealed at Pompeii by the eruption of 79, the palaestra column found by Matteo della Corte in 1936 and the fragment he had seen in 1925 in the house of Publius Paquius Proculus. Others come from Cirencester, from Dura-Europos before its fall in 256, and from Roman Manchester. Felix Grosser showed in 1926, in the Archiv für Religionswissenschaft, that the letters rearrange into two PATER NOSTERs crossed at a shared N, with two As and two Os left over to be read as alpha and omega, and took the square for a Christian cryptogram; the Pompeian date makes that difficult. Amedeo Maiuri argued for a literary game or a pre-Christian charm, and Nicolas Vinel has more recently made a case for a Jewish origin. AREPO, which occurs nowhere else in Latin, is the knot in every reading.","Whatever it was, it worked for a very long time. The square was copied onto amulets, bread-moulds, wells, bowls and church walls from Roman Britain to Ethiopia, prescribed against fire, fever, madness and snakebite, and was still being cut in the nineteenth century; in Ethiopian tradition the five words are turned into a set of names — Sador, Alador, Danat, Adera, Rodas — given to the nails or the wounds of Christ. The acronymic habit outlived it. INRI, the titulus of John 19:19, was reread by alchemical and Rosicrucian writers as Igne Natura Renovatur Integra, by fire nature is renewed entire; VITRIOL, lettered around the Azoth figure printed at Frankfurt in 1613 under Basil Valentine's name, spells out an instruction and the name of a solvent at once, and sometimes acquires an eighth letter for veram medicinam. Neither rereading has a traceable first author, and both circulate for two centuries without one. The name for the technique — notariacon — was itself imported from Hebrew by the Christian Kabbalists, which is the whole matter in a word: Latin had to borrow even the term for what it was doing with its own letters."],terms:[{native:"notariacon",translit:"notariacon",gloss:"the Latin spelling of Hebrew notariqon, reading each letter of a word as the initial of another; borrowed by the Christian Kabbalists because Latin had no native name for the art"},{native:"INRI",translit:"INRI",gloss:"Iesus Nazarenus Rex Iudaeorum, the titulus of John 19:19; reread in alchemical and Rosicrucian writing as Igne Natura Renovatur Integra, a rereading with no identifiable author"},{native:"VITRIOL",translit:"VITRIOL",gloss:"Visita Interiora Terrae Rectificando Invenies Occultum Lapidem; in print by 1613 around the Azoth emblem, and sometimes extended by a final M for veram medicinam"},{native:"TENET",translit:"TENET",gloss:"'he holds'; the middle word of the SATOR square, a palindrome in itself, forming an equal-armed cross through the grid with a T at each of its four ends"},{native:"hoc est enim corpus meum",translit:"hoc est enim corpus meum",gloss:"'for this is my body'; the words of consecration in the Roman Canon, and the supposed origin of hocus pocus — an etymology proposed in 1694 and accepted by nobody who studies the word"},{native:"abecedarium",translit:"abecedarium",gloss:"the alphabet written out; in the rite for dedicating a church the bishop traces the Greek and Latin alphabets in ashes across the floor in a diagonal cross"}],texts:[{title:"Missale Romanum (1570)",note:"The Tridentine missal, fixing the Canon and placing the prologue of John at the end of the rite as the Last Gospel — the text most often carried as an amulet in the Latin Middle Ages."},{title:"Pontificale Romanum, the rite for the dedication of a church",note:"The bishop traces the Greek and Latin alphabets in ashes on the floor in a diagonal cross: the letters themselves used to take possession of a building."},{title:"The SATOR square: Pompeii, Cirencester, Dura-Europos, Manchester",note:"Two Pompeian examples sealed by 79 CE; the Dura examples fall before 256. The corpus is the evidence against which every proposed reading has to be tested, and none has yet passed."},{title:"Felix Grosser, in Archiv für Religionswissenschaft 24 (1926)",note:"The PATER NOSTER anagram, with the two As and two Os read as alpha and omega. The most influential reading of the square, and hard to reconcile with a pre-79 date."},{title:"John Tillotson, sermon printed 1694",note:"The derivation of hocus pocus from hoc est corpus. Endlessly repeated, rejected by lexicographers, and a useful example of how a good story outruns its evidence."}]},{slug:"defixiones",title:"The Binding Tablets",native:"Defixiones",translit:"defixiones",summary:"Sheets of lead, folded, nailed and dropped into graves and springs — the largest body of Latin magical writing that survives, and not one line of it literary.",paragraphs:["The object comes before the text. Lead was cheap, soft enough to inscribe with a stylus, cold and heavy, and associated with the underworld; sheets were hammered thin, written on, rolled or folded, frequently pierced with a nail, and deposited where the chthonic powers could be reached — a grave, a well, a spring, a sanctuary boundary. More than a thousand are known from across the Roman world, in Greek and in Latin. Auguste Audollent's Defixionum tabellae of 1904 is the founding corpus, Amina Kropp's Latin corpus of 2008 the current one, and John Gager's Curse Tablets and Binding Spells from the Ancient World (1992) the readable door in English. They sort by purpose with depressing clarity: lawsuits, love and its refusals, rivals in trade, athletes and charioteers, and thieves.","The governing verb is defigo, to fix down or pin, and the tablets frequently do to the writing what they want done to the person. Names are written backwards; individual letters are reversed; lines run in alternate directions; the sheet is folded shut and driven through with a nail. The formulae are those of legal transfer — do, dono, dedico, mando, I give, I hand over, I consign — so that the victim becomes the property of a god and the god's business to deal with. The addressees are Dis Pater, Proserpina, Mercury and the nameless dead of the grave the tablet was buried in; and from the imperial period onward Greek, Egyptian and Jewish names appear naturalised into Latin texts — Iao, Abrasax, Adonai — because a curse-writer used whatever names had a reputation, without regard for whose they were.","The richest single deposit is British. About one hundred and thirty tablets came from the sacred spring of Sulis Minerva at Bath, published by Roger Tomlin in 1988. Most are not curses in the ordinary sense but what Henk Versnel labelled prayers for justice: a theft is reported to the goddess, the stolen property is sometimes formally made over to her so that the crime becomes hers to avenge, and the unknown culprit is covered by an exhaustive formula — whether man or woman, whether slave or free. They are badly spelled, and they are the closest thing we have to the unmediated voice of ordinary provincials in Roman Britain. One of them may not be in Latin at all: Tomlin identified a text he took to be written in British Celtic in Latin letters, which if he is right is the only surviving document in that language.","The law had views. The Twelve Tables already legislated against the malum carmen and against charming a neighbour's crops across the boundary — the ancestor, at five centuries' remove, of every later statute against maleficium. Pliny devotes a long passage of the Natural History (XXVIII.10–29) to whether words have power and declines to settle it, observing that the wisest men individually disbelieve it and collectively behave otherwise; and at XVIII.41–43 he tells of C. Furius Cresimus, a freedman prosecuted for luring away his neighbours' harvests, who brought his implements, his oxen and his daughter into court, said that these were his charms, and was acquitted. Under the Empire the charge is veneficium, prosecuted under the lex Cornelia de sicariis et veneficis, and Apuleius' Apologia, delivered at Sabratha about 158, is the only complete speech from a Roman magic trial that survives.","For the historian the tablets are valuable precisely because they are not literature. Nobody wrote them to be read, or to expound a doctrine, or to impress a patron; they are the residue of ordinary fury, produced by people who mostly could not write well, and they are the least filtered evidence Latin has for what magic was actually used for. The vocabulary they leave behind records judgements rather than definitions — carmen means both poem and spell, veneficium both poisoning and sorcery, and magus is a Persian priestly title turned into a criminal charge — and the modern category 'magic' has inherited those judgements without always noticing. The practice does not stop at the end of antiquity, either: inscribed lead continues into the medieval centuries, which is a continuity worth stating and hard to trace, since the later examples are scattered and no corpus of them exists on the scale of Audollent's."],terms:[{native:"defixio",translit:"defixio",gloss:"a binding-curse, and the tablet that carries it; the scholarly name is modern, built from the verb the tablets themselves use"},{native:"defigo",translit:"defigo",gloss:"to fix down, to pin, to drive in; the operative verb, and one the tablets enact physically with a nail through the folded sheet"},{native:"tabella",translit:"tabella",gloss:"a small sheet or leaf; the lead support, hammered thin, on which nearly the whole surviving corpus is written"},{native:"carmen",translit:"carmen",gloss:"song, poem, spell — one word for all three. The Twelve Tables legislate against the malum carmen, and English 'charm' descends from it through French"},{native:"veneficium",translit:"veneficium",gloss:"poisoning and sorcery in a single word; the charge under the lex Cornelia, and a reminder that Roman law never separated the two offences"},{native:"magus",translit:"magus",gloss:"a Persian priest, then a foreign wonder-worker, then an accusation; Apuleius spends a long speech refusing the title"}],texts:[{title:"Auguste Audollent, Defixionum tabellae (Paris, 1904)",note:"The founding corpus of curse tablets, Greek and Latin; still the reference by which individual texts are cited."},{title:"R. S. O. Tomlin, 'The Curse Tablets', in B. Cunliffe (ed.), The Temple of Sulis Minerva at Bath, vol. 2 (1988)",note:"About one hundred and thirty tablets from the sacred spring, mostly prayers for justice over stolen property; includes the text Tomlin took to be British Celtic."},{title:"Pliny, Natural History XXVIII.10–29 and XVIII.41–43",note:"The long discussion of whether words have power, left open; and the acquittal of C. Furius Cresimus, who produced his tools and oxen in court as his charms."},{title:"Apuleius, Apologia",note:"Delivered at Sabratha about 158 before the proconsul Claudius Maximus; the only complete surviving speech from a Roman trial for magic, and a defendant's own account of what the charge meant."},{title:"John G. Gager, Curse Tablets and Binding Spells from the Ancient World (1992)",note:"A large selection in English translation, arranged by purpose, with the ancient legal and social context set out."}]},{slug:"cabala-christiana",title:"Kabbalah in Latin",native:"Cabala",translit:"Cabala",summary:"A twenty-three-year-old hired a Sicilian convert to translate a library of Hebrew mysticism into Latin, and invented a Christian Kabbalah that has not stopped being written since.",paragraphs:["In 1486 Pico della Mirandola employed Flavius Mithridates — Guglielmo Raimondo Moncada, a Sicilian Jew who had converted and taught Hebrew in Rome — to translate a large body of Hebrew and Aramaic kabbalistic writing into Latin for his use. Chaim Wirszubski's Pico della Mirandola's Encounter with Jewish Mysticism (1989) identified the translations, showed that Pico read Kabbalah almost entirely through them, and showed further that Mithridates interpolated Christological readings into his versions as he went. That is the single most important fact about everything downstream: the Kabbalah Pico met was already Christianised in the act of translation, before he had begun to argue anything from it.","The theses put it to work. Among the nine hundred stand forty-seven cabalistic conclusions drawn from the Hebrew sages and seventy-one according to Pico's own opinion, including the assertion that no science more certainly assures us of Christ's divinity than magic and Kabbalah. His characteristic move is to take the letter-techniques and turn them on Christian doctrine — most famously by inserting the letter shin into the Tetragrammaton to yield a pentagrammaton read as the name of Jesus, a demonstration Johann Reuchlin built out at length in De verbo mirifico of 1494. The 1487 condemnation fell on this material along with the rest: thirteen theses censured by the commission in February, all nine hundred by the bull of August, absolution only in 1493. S. A. Farmer's edition of 1998 prints the theses with their sources and is where the argument can now be followed.","Reuchlin's De arte cabalistica of 1517 — a dialogue in three books among a Jew, a Pythagorean and a Muslim, dedicated to Leo X — is the first systematic Christian exposition, and it was written in the middle of a fight. From 1510 Reuchlin had defended Hebrew books against Johannes Pfefferkorn and the Cologne theologians who wanted them confiscated and destroyed, and the affair became a European cause, satirised on his side in the Epistolae obscurorum virorum of 1515–17. The defence of the Hebrew library and the appropriation of the Hebrew tradition are two faces of one movement, and the tension between them is nowhere resolved in the corpus. It should be said plainly that the enterprise was in large part conversionary in intent, and that its Jewish sources were being read for conclusions their authors did not hold.","The carriers after Reuchlin are all Latin. Francesco Giorgi, a Venetian Franciscan, built a cosmos of sefirot and musical proportion in De harmonia mundi totius (Venice, 1525). Agrippa's third book absorbed the material into a working handbook. Guillaume Postel translated the Sefer Yetzirah into Latin (Paris, 1552) and worked on the Zohar besides. Kircher gathered a vast and idiosyncratic collection into the Oedipus Aegyptiacus (1652–54). Then Christian Knorr von Rosenroth's Kabbala Denudata — Sulzbach 1677–78, Frankfurt 1684 — set the Idrot and the Sifra di-Tzeni'uta of the Zohar, Lurianic doctrine out of Vital, and Abraham Cohen de Herrera's Porta Coelorum before readers with no Hebrew at all, with lexicons and dissertations to steer them. Gershom Scholem judged the translations inaccurate and the influence enormous; both judgements are correct, and Allison Coudert has traced how much Christian doctrine the arrangement carries in with it.","What the Latin did to the words is the lasting consequence. Almost every term the modern West uses reached it through a transliteration made by Christians for Christian purposes — sephiroth, Ensoph, Malchuth, and the spelling cabala itself, which is why the same word is written three ways in English books. Mathers englished portions of Knorr in 1887 as The Kabbalah Unveiled, and by that road the sefirot reached the Golden Dawn, at several removes from Safed and with the Latin intermediary invisible to the people using it. Two cautions close this current. 'Christian Kabbalah' is a modern scholarly label and not a name anyone in it used. And the Jewish tradition from which this material was taken is a living one, whose own history is not the subject here; what is described above is what Latin readers made of what reached them, which is a different thing and was frequently a distortion of it."],terms:[{native:"Cabala",translit:"Cabala",gloss:"the Latin spelling used by Pico and Reuchlin; Hebrew qabbalah, 'that which is received', and the source of the competing English spellings in use today"},{native:"sephiroth",translit:"sephiroth",gloss:"the ten emanations; transliterated rather than translated, and so entering Latin as an untranslatable technical term — which is how it entered every later Western language"},{native:"Ensoph",translit:"Ensoph",gloss:"the Latin form of ein sof, 'without end'; the unlimited from which the sefirot proceed, and the point at which Christian expositors most often reach for Neoplatonic vocabulary"},{native:"revolutio alphabetaria",translit:"revolutio alphabetaria",gloss:"Pico's Latin for the art of letter-permutation, Hebrew temurah; one of three techniques he takes over, with notariacon and gematria"},{native:"pentagrammaton",translit:"pentagrammaton",gloss:"the five-letter name got by inserting shin into the Tetragrammaton and read as the name of Jesus; the demonstration on which Christian Kabbalah's claim to prove Christian doctrine chiefly rested"},{native:"verbum mirificum",translit:"verbum mirificum",gloss:"the wonder-working word; the title and the subject of Reuchlin's book of 1494, and the Christian counterpart to the ineffable Name"}],texts:[{title:"Pico della Mirandola, Conclusiones cabalisticae (in the 900 theses, Rome, 1486)",note:"Forty-seven conclusions from the Hebrew sages and seventy-one of his own opinion. Edited with the rest by S. A. Farmer, Syncretism in the West (1998)."},{title:"Chaim Wirszubski, Pico della Mirandola's Encounter with Jewish Mysticism (1989)",note:"Identifies the translations Flavius Mithridates made for Pico and shows the Christological interpolations he introduced into them — the foundation of all later work on the subject."},{title:"Johann Reuchlin, De arte cabalistica (1517)",note:"A dialogue among a Jew, a Pythagorean and a Muslim; the first systematic Christian exposition, written in the middle of Reuchlin's fight to prevent the destruction of Hebrew books."},{title:"Francesco Giorgi, De harmonia mundi totius (Venice, 1525)",note:"A Franciscan's cosmos assembled from sefirot and musical proportion; the route by which kabbalistic structure entered Renaissance architecture and cosmology."},{title:"Christian Knorr von Rosenroth, Kabbala Denudata (Sulzbach 1677–78; Frankfurt 1684)",note:"The Zohar's Idrot and Sifra di-Tzeni'uta, Lurianic material from Vital, and Herrera's Porta Coelorum, in Latin with lexicons. Loose, selective, Christian in framing, and the source of nearly all later Western usage."}]},{slug:"lingua-graduum",title:"The Latin of the Lodges",native:"Post CXX annos patebo",translit:"Post CXX annos patebo",summary:"By 1888 almost nobody in these orders could read Latin — which is precisely when it became the language of their grades, their mottoes and their tomb inscriptions.",paragraphs:["The Rosicrucian manifestos divide their languages along a line typical of the period. The Fama Fraternitatis was printed at Kassel in 1614 in German; the Confessio Fraternitatis at Kassel in 1615 in Latin, bound with an exposition of Dee's Monas by one 'Philippus a Gabella'; the Chymische Hochzeit at Strasbourg in 1616, again in German. The movement announced itself in the vernacular and argued itself in Latin. The Fama's central scene is a vault: seven-sided, lit without sun, the founder's body uncorrupted at its centre, the door inscribed POST CXX ANNOS PATEBO — after a hundred and twenty years I shall open — and the walls carrying EX DEO NASCIMUR, IN IESU MORIMUR, PER SPIRITUM SANCTUM REVIVISCIMUS. That is Latin doing what an inscription does. It is not being reasoned in; it is being read off a wall.","The chronology has to be given twice. The manifestos' own account: Christian Rosenkreuz born in 1378, taught at Damcar in Arabia and in Fez and Egypt, dead in 1484 in his hundred and sixth year, his vault opened in 1604 at exactly the promised interval. The philological account places the composition in the Tübingen circle around Johann Valentin Andreae about 1610 — Adam Haslmayr saw a manuscript Fama in Tyrol in that year, and his printed reply of 1612 is the earliest external witness to it, a reply that cost him four and a half years on the Genoese galleys. Andreae acknowledged only the Chymische Hochzeit and called it a ludibrium, a jest. How much of the other two is his has been argued by Roland Edighoffer, Carlos Gilly and John Warwick Montgomery and is not settled.","The grades came later and by a different road. In 1710 a book printed at Breslau under the name Sincerus Renatus — Samuel Richter — gave rules for an Order of the Golden and Rosy Cross, though whether any order stood behind it is doubted. By the 1750s a body of that name was working inside German Freemasonry, requiring its candidates to be Master Masons, and by 1777 it had settled into nine grades: Junior, Theoreticus, Practicus, Philosophus, Adeptus Minor, Adeptus Major, Adeptus Exemptus, Magister, Magus. Those names passed to the Societas Rosicruciana in Anglia, founded in 1866, and thence in 1888 to the Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn, which fitted them to the sefirot and added Ipsissimus at the summit. Every one of the words had been an ordinary Latin noun: an adeptus was a man who had actually achieved the Stone, a magister a university teacher, a magus a Persian priest. As grade-names they cease to describe anything and become positions on a ladder.","Members took Latin names as well. Wynn Westcott used Sapere aude, Horace's dare to know; Mathers took Deo duce comite ferro, with God as leader and the sword as companion; W. B. Yeats took Demon est Deus inversus, itself lifted from Blavatsky; Aleister Crowley took Perdurabo, I shall endure to the end; Florence Farr took Sapientia sapienti dono data. Freemasonry's Latin is of the same kind — Ordo ab chao, Audi vide tace, Deus meumque ius — and so is the Latin on lodge furniture and diplomas. None of it is composed Latin in the humanist sense. It is Latin as insignia: chosen for weight, for brevity, for the fact that it can be inscribed, and not least for the fact that an outsider cannot read it at a glance.","There is a loss recorded in that, and it is the end of the story this hall tells. The Golden Dawn's sources were overwhelmingly Latin — Knorr's Kabbala Denudata, Agrippa, the Clavicula manuscripts, Kircher — and its members overwhelmingly could not read them; Mathers translated, and his translations are where the order's Latin actually resided, along with his errors. From 1144, when Latin was the only way into this material at all, to 1888, when it survives as a set of titles arranged down a diagram, is the whole arc of the language in Western esotericism. It is not, however, a closed one. Freemasonry, the Societas Rosicruciana in Anglia and orders in the Golden Dawn's line are living organisations with living members, their grade-names and mottoes are in present use, and what is set out above is where that vocabulary came from rather than any judgement on what is done with it now."],terms:[{native:"Post CXX annos patebo",translit:"Post CXX annos patebo",gloss:"'After 120 years I shall open'; the inscription on the door of Christian Rosenkreuz's vault in the Fama, and the hinge of the whole Rosicrucian chronology"},{native:"Ex Deo nascimur, in Iesu morimur, per Spiritum Sanctum reviviscimus",translit:"Ex Deo nascimur, in Iesu morimur, per Spiritum Sanctum reviviscimus",gloss:"'From God we are born, in Jesus we die, through the Holy Spirit we live again'; the vault's wall inscription, and the manifestos' most quoted sentence"},{native:"Adeptus Minor",translit:"Adeptus Minor",gloss:"the first of the adept grades in the Gold- und Rosenkreuz scheme of 1777 and in the Golden Dawn after it; in alchemy an adeptus had been simply a man who had achieved the Stone"},{native:"Sapere aude",translit:"Sapere aude",gloss:"'Dare to know'; Horace, Epistles I.2, taken by William Wynn Westcott as his Golden Dawn motto and by Kant, a century earlier, as the motto of the Enlightenment"},{native:"Perdurabo",translit:"Perdurabo",gloss:"'I shall endure to the end'; Aleister Crowley's motto on entering the Golden Dawn in 1898, and the name under which he published for the rest of his life"},{native:"Ordo ab chao",translit:"Ordo ab chao",gloss:"'Order out of chaos'; the motto of the Ancient and Accepted Scottish Rite, and a fair sample of how Masonic Latin works — three words, no syntax to speak of, maximum weight"}],texts:[{title:"Fama Fraternitatis (Kassel, 1614)",note:"In German. The story of the brotherhood and the opening of the vault, with the Latin inscriptions that the tradition has quoted ever since."},{title:"Confessio Fraternitatis (Kassel, 1615)",note:"In Latin, bound with the Consideratio brevis of 'Philippus a Gabella', an exposition of Dee's Monas Hieroglyphica; the only one of the three manifestos in the language of this hall."},{title:"Sincerus Renatus [Samuel Richter], Die wahrhaffte und vollkommene Bereitung des Philosophischen Steins (Breslau, 1710)",note:"The first printed rules for an Order of the Golden and Rosy Cross; whether any such order then existed is doubted, and the book may describe a body as fictional as the Fama's."},{title:"The Golden Dawn Cipher Manuscripts",note:"The founding documents of the order of 1888, in Trithemius's cipher alphabet and in English, with the grade structure and sefirotic attributions; the accompanying 'Anna Sprengel' correspondence is in German and is generally judged a fabrication."},{title:"Israel Regardie, The Golden Dawn (Chicago, 1937–1940)",note:"Four volumes publishing the order's rituals and knowledge lectures against the wishes of its surviving members; the fullest printed record of what the Latin grade-names were attached to."}]}]};export{e as default};
